  "mechanisms": [
    {
      "id": "mechanism_1",
      "name": "Suzuki segregation",
      "category": "segregation",
      "sample_id": "sample_1",
      "material_state": "crept",
      "assertion_level": "author_interpretation",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": 3,
          "section": null,
          "quote": "There are few previous reports of Suzuki segregation on extended stacking faults in other alloys [14–17].",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "98b1b06ef18d65b9:part:001"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism_2",
      "name": "long-range diffusion",
      "category": "diffusion",
      "sample_id": "sample_2",
      "material_state": "crept",
      "assertion_level": "author_hypothesis",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": 3,
          "section": null,
          "quote": "The segregation behavior observed presently suggests that long-range diﬀusion of elements is also intimately associated with these shearing processes.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "98b1b06ef18d65b9:part:001"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism_3",
      "name": "vacancy-mediated reordering",
      "category": "diffusion",
      "sample_id": "sample_2",
      "material_state": "crept",
      "assertion_level": "author_hypothesis",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": 3,
          "section": null,
          "quote": "The hypothesis forwarded is that vacancy-mediated reordering can occur between adjacent anti phase boundary (APB)/CSF faults, thereby forming an SISF, while reordering a two-layer CSF will result in an SESF.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "98b1b06ef18d65b9:part:001"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "mechanism_4",
      "name": "diffusion along dislocation cores",
      "category": "diffusion",
      "sample_id": "sample_2",
      "material_state": "crept",
      "assertion_level": "author_hypothesis",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": 4,
          "section": null,
          "quote": "A third possibility is that there is indeed diﬀusion to/from the c channels occurring along the dislocation lines of the shearing partials. Faster diﬀusion rates (and distances) along the dislocation cores of partial dislocations may also provide natural conduits for ﬂow of atomic species necessary to lower the energy of the faults and enable shearing to take place.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "98b1b06ef18d65b9:part:001"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
